      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p><strong>An IPC audit in a care home checks the environment for infection control: hand hygiene, PPE, and cleaning and cleanability. You test it against the code of practice on infections. Quickler records your walk in WhatsApp with photos. There is no new app. It is not a care records or clinical system.</strong></p><p>Infection spreads by hands, surfaces, shared equipment and waste. The audit walks those routes before an outbreak does. The question is how to record the walk so the empty dispenser and the overdue clean are caught that day.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>An IPC audit checks the care environment against the code of practice on infections, made under the Health and Social Care Act 2008.</li><li>Three areas: hand hygiene, PPE, and cleaning and cleanability.</li><li>It checks the place and the process. It is not a resident's clinical record.</li><li>The audit does not stop an outbreak. Acting on it does. The registered manager owns the actions.</li><li>Rules vary across the UK. Use the framework for your home.</li><li>Quickler records the walk and gives the group a live dashboard of every home.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The standard",
      "heading": "The code of practice on infections",
      "html": "<p>In England, IPC in registered care sits under the code of practice on infections. It is made under the Health and Social Care Act 2008 and linked to the CQC fundamental standards. A provider needs systems to manage infection risk, a clean environment, and the policies, training and resources to back them. CQC assesses IPC under the safe key question.</p><p>The audit shows the home meets the code in practice, not only on paper. Guidance changes and varies by nation. Use the current code and your regulator's guidance. This is general information, not clinical or legal advice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Hand hygiene and PPE",
      "heading": "Hand hygiene and PPE",
      "html": "<p>Hand hygiene is the most important IPC control. Check the basics are there: basins with soap and paper towels, alcohol gel where care happens, no blocked or broken dispensers. Then watch what staff do. PPE is next: gloves, aprons, masks and eye protection, in stock, stored well, in date, and put on, taken off and thrown away properly.</p><p>Record each as you see it, with a photo of the empty dispenser or the well-stocked station. Quickler holds what staff were seen doing. It holds no data about any resident.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Environment and scope",
      "heading": "Environment, and the line we keep",
      "html": "<p>The third area is the place itself. Cleaning schedules followed. Surfaces and floors whole, not cracked or worn. Equipment cleaned between residents. Waste and laundry kept apart and handled safely. Take a photo of anything that cannot be cleaned and needs replacing.</p><p>The line: Quickler checks the place and the process. It is not a clinical record. It does not manage infection cases, care plans or medication. That stays in your care system. The group dashboard lets a regional IPC lead see every home at once.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>What is an IPC audit in a care home?</h3><p>A check of the care home environment for infection prevention and control. It covers hand hygiene, PPE, and cleaning and cleanability. It tests the home against the code of practice under the Health and Social Care Act 2008. CQC assesses IPC under the safe key question.</p><h3>How often should a care home do an IPC audit?</h3><p>The law sets no single rate. Many homes audit monthly or every three months, with daily cleaning checks. They audit more often in an outbreak. Follow the home's IPC policy and the current code.</p><h3>What does an IPC audit checklist cover?</h3><p>Hand hygiene: basins, soap, paper towels, alcohol gel, working dispensers, and what staff actually do. PPE: gloves, aprons, masks and eye protection, in stock, stored well, in date, used and disposed of properly. Environment: cleaning schedules followed, surfaces that can be cleaned, safe waste and laundry.</p><h3>Does Quickler manage infection cases or medication?</h3><p>No. Quickler checks the place and the process, with photos. It is not a clinical record. Infection cases, care plans and medication stay in your care system.</p><h3>Can I run an IPC audit in WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Each prompt arrives in your WhatsApp chat. You answer by text, voice note or photo. When you say done, Quickler writes the report and the action list. No new app. No login. No resident data.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-audit-software-uk.html\">Care home audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/cqc-compliance-audit-checklist-uk.html\">CQC compliance audit checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-home-health-safety-audit-uk.html\">Care home health and safety audit</a></li></ul>"
